@@ -0,0 +1,49 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-json-patch-wrap-pointer-errors-as-jsonpatcherror-sequential-move-semantics-no-deep-clone-on-copy
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Patch: wrap pointer errors as JsonPatchError, sequential move semantics, no deep-clone on copy"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` (RFC 6902) is built on top of the existing `resolvePointer`/
+`JsonPointerError` (RFC 6901, dl-001). RFC 6902 leaves some implementation choices open: how to surface
+pointer-resolution failures to `applyPatch` callers, how exactly `move` interacts with array index
+shifts, whether `copy` needs to isolate the copied subtree, and how to treat the `-` array token outside
+of `add`.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `resolvePointer`'s `JsonPointerError`, raised while resolving a `path` or `from`, is caught inside
+  `applyPatch` and re-wrapped as `JsonPatchError`. `JsonPatchError` is the only error type `applyPatch`
+  callers need to catch; they are not expected to reach into pointer-level errors for a patch-level
+  operation. This mirrors dl-001's preference for one error class per public operation rather than a
+  taxonomy of causes.
+- `move` is implemented literally as the RFC describes it: a `remove` at `from`, followed by an `add` at
+  `path` against the document *as already modified by that removal* — not against the original
+  document. This matches RFC 6902 Appendix A.7 (moving `/foo/1` to `/foo/3` in a 4-element array lands
+  the moved element at the end, because the removal shifts the later indices first). Implementations
+  that instead resolve `path` against the pre-removal document would disagree with the RFC's own
+  worked example.
+- `copy` does not deep-clone the value it reads from `from` before writing it at `path`. The library's
+  immutable-update strategy is copy-on-write (shallow-copy only the containers on the write path); it
+  never mutates an existing node in place. So two locations sharing the same subtree reference cannot
+  cause one to change through later writes to the other — deep-cloning would only add cost, not safety.
+- The `-` array token is accepted only as the last token of an `add` operation's `path` (meaning "append
+  after the last element"). It is rejected as an intermediate path segment, as the last token of
+  `replace`/`remove`, and anywhere in a `from` pointer — consistent with dl-001's reasoning that `-`
+  never identifies an existing array element, which is also why plain `resolvePointer` (used for `test`
+  and for reading `from`) already rejects it unconditionally.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers of `applyPatch` get a single `JsonPatchError` type for every failure (bad patch shape, failed
+  operation, failed `test`); they cannot distinguish a pointer-syntax error from a resolution failure
+  without inspecting the message, same tradeoff as dl-001 made for `resolvePointer`.
+- Because `move`'s `path` is resolved after `from` is removed, a patch author must reason about `path`
+  in terms of the post-removal document when both point into the same array — this is surprising only if
+  one expects `path` to be independent of `from`, which the RFC itself does not promise.
+- Sharing subtree references between `copy`'s source and destination is only safe as long as every write
+  operation in this module stays copy-on-write (no in-place mutation). Any future operation added to this
+  module must preserve that invariant, or `copy` will need to start deep-cloning.
