@@ -0,0 +1,57 @@
+---
+id: task-002-implement-json-patch-rfc-6902-applypatch
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Patch RFC 6902 applyPatch"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## What was done
+
+Added `applyPatch(document, patch)`, exported from `src/index.ts`, implementing RFC 6902 JSON Patch:
+
+- Supports all six operations: `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, `test`.
+- Validates the patch's structure at runtime (array of operation objects, known `op`, required
+  `path`/`value`/`from` per operation, per the same "validate external input" convention already used
+  by `resolvePointer`), throwing the new `JsonPatchError` (exported alongside `applyPatch`) for any
+  structural problem or failed operation. Underlying `JsonPointerError`s raised while resolving a `path`
+  or `from` are caught and re-wrapped as `JsonPatchError`, so callers only need to catch one error type
+  for `applyPatch`.
+- Applies operations immutably via copy-on-write: each write only shallow-copies the containers along
+  the target path, structurally sharing the rest of the document with the input. The input `document` is
+  never mutated.
+- Reuses the existing pointer-parsing helpers (`parsePointer`, `parseArrayIndex`, `isJsonObject`) from
+  the RFC 6901 implementation already in this module, rather than re-deriving pointer syntax.
+- `move` is implemented as the RFC prescribes: a `remove` at `from` followed immediately by an `add` at
+  `path` against the *already-modified* document, which is what makes moving an element to a later index
+  within the same array land at the right place (verified against RFC 6902 Appendix A.7). `move` also
+  rejects a `from` that is a proper prefix of `path` (moving a value into one of its own children), per
+  the RFC; moving a value to itself is allowed (not a *proper* prefix).
+- `copy` does not deep-clone the copied value — safe because copy-on-write never mutates an existing
+  node in place, only replaces references on the path being written, so sharing the subtree between two
+  locations cannot cause one to change through the other later.
+- `test` uses a structural deep-equal (RFC 6902 §4.6 semantics: same type, same array length/order,
+  same object keys regardless of order, recursively equal values).
+- The `-` array token is only accepted as the last token of `add`'s `path` (append semantics); it is
+  rejected everywhere else (as an intermediate navigation step, or as the last token for `replace`/
+  `remove`, or anywhere in `from`), consistent with dl-001's reasoning that `-` never identifies an
+  existing array element.
+
+Test-first: extended `src/index.test.ts` with the full set of RFC 6902 Appendix A examples (A.1–A.12,
+A.14–A.16; confirmed red against the missing `applyPatch` export, then green), plus additional
+happy-path coverage (`copy`, replacing the whole document at the root path, chaining several operations)
+and edge/error-path coverage (invalid patch shape, unknown op, missing `value`/`from`, removing the
+whole document, `move` into own child, `-` misuse, out-of-bounds index, missing member for
+`replace`/`remove`, and a non-mutation check against a snapshot of the input).
+
+Verified `src/index.ts` type-checks cleanly under the project's strict `tsconfig.json`, using the same
+one-off temporary `typescript` install approach as task-001 (still no `typecheck` script wired into the
+repo — pre-existing gap, not introduced here).
+
+Documented `applyPatch` in `README.md` under "API", alongside `resolvePointer`.
+
+## Follow-ups (not done here, out of scope for this task)
+
+- Same pre-existing gaps noted in task-001: no `typescript`/`@types/node` devDependencies or
+  `typecheck` script, and the `.ts`-extension-in-relative-imports convention needed for
+  `node --test`'s native type stripping is not reconciled with `tsc`/NodeNext's `.js`-extension
+  convention.
